/-!
# Convergent q-expansion tends to its constant term at i∞

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.tendsto_atImInfty_of_hasSum_qParam`
(`99e3990b-a664-51c3-aced-4302ef14c172`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `f78ca6b2-75b2-5d34-b8df-4d0ef380ad0f`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $h$ be a real number with $0 < h$, let $F \colon \mathfrak{H} \to \mathbb{C}$ be an arbitrary
function on the upper half-plane, and let $(c_m)_{m \in \mathbb{N}}$ be a sequence of complex
numbers. Assume that for every $\tau \in \mathfrak{H}$ the series $\sum_{m \ge 0} c_m\, q^m$, where
$q = \mathrm{qParam}\,h\,\tau = e^{2\pi i \tau / h}$ is Mathlib's $q$-parameter of period $h$, is
summable with sum exactly $F(\tau)$ (the hypothesis is `HasSum`, i.e. unconditional summability of
the family indexed by $m \in \mathbb{N}$ together with the identification of its sum with $F(\tau)$;
no hypothesis of local uniformity or of holomorphy of $F$ is imposed). The conclusion is that $F$
converges to $c_0$ along the filter `UpperHalfPlane.atImInfty`, the filter generated by the sets on
which $\operatorname{Im} \tau$ exceeds a given bound: that is, $F(\tau) \to c_0$ as
$\operatorname{Im} \tau \to \infty$.

This is the standard statement that a function on the upper half-plane given by a convergent
$q$-expansion of period $h$ is holomorphic-at-the-cusp-style bounded at $i\infty$, with limiting
value the constant term $c_0$; in particular such an $F$ is bounded near $i\infty$. It is used in
the treatment of Siegel units and in the estimates bounding slashed modular functions in terms of
$q$-parameters.

